{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 924 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "53d0990c5e531826e4379dcde554dfc074761e0975e72fd8e4c3a09b5ce7a501" :edb/db-before-t 923 :edb/db-after-t 924 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534236 50 #inst "2026-10-11T18:06:24.114Z" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186151865 1071 "Is the following statement true or false?\n\n> The integer $21$ is the additive inverse of $14$ modulo $35$." 13194139534236 17592186045417 false] [17592186151865 1071 "Is the following statement true or false?\n\nThe integer $21$ is the additive inverse of $14$ modulo $35.$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186151865 1074 "The additive inverse of $14$ modulo $35$ is the integer\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\lt 35\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\nx + 14 \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:35)\n$$\n\nNow, notice that when we add our two numbers, we get a number that is congruent to zero modulo $35{:}$\n\n$$\n21 + 14 \\mid ≡35\\begin{vmatrix}(mod35) \\\\ ≡0\\end{vmatrix}(mod35)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the statement is true." 13194139534236 17592186045417 false] [17592186151865 1074 "The additive inverse of $14$ modulo $35$ is the integer $0 \\leq x \\lt 35$ such that\n\n$$\nx + 14 \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:35).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that when we add our two numbers, we get a number that is congruent to zero modulo $35{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21+14 & ≡35 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,35) \\\\  & ≡0 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the statement is true." 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186151866 1071 "Find the additive inverse of $17$ modulo $26$." 13194139534236 17592186045417 false] [17592186151866 1071 "Find the additive inverse of $17$ modulo $26.$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186151866 1074 "The additive inverse of $17$ modulo $26$ is the integer\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\lt 26\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\nx + 17 \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:26)\n$$\n\nNotice that $-17 + 17 \\equiv 0$, so $x\\equiv -17\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:26)$. To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\nx \\mid ≡ - 17\\begin{vmatrix}(mod26) \\\\ ≡0 - 17\\end{vmatrix}(mod26); ≡26 - 17\\begin{vmatrix}(mod26) \\\\ ≡9\\end{vmatrix}(mod26)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $17$ modulo $26$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9}$.\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n$9 + 17 \\equiv 26 \\equiv 0\\:\\:{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}} \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:26)$\n\n**Note:** The additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n$, i.e. $-a \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: n$." 13194139534236 17592186045417 false] [17592186151866 1074 "The additive inverse of $17$ modulo $26$ is the integer $0 \\leq x \\lt 26$ such that\n\n$$\nx + 17 \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:26).\n$$\n\nNotice that $-17 + 17 \\equiv 0,$ so $x\\equiv -17\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:26).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-17 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,26) \\\\  & ≡0-17 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,26) \\\\  & ≡26-17 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,26) \\\\  & ≡9 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,26)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $17$ modulo $26$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9}.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n9 + 17 \\equiv 26 \\equiv 0\\:\\:{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}} \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:26)\n$$\n\n**Note:** The additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: n.$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186151867 1071 "Find the additive inverse of $23$ modulo $15$." 13194139534236 17592186045417 false] [17592186151867 1071 "Find the additive inverse of $23$ modulo $15.$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186151867 1074 "The additive inverse of $23$ modulo $15$ is the integer\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\lt 15\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\nx + 23 \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:15)\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $23\\equiv 8\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15){:}$\n$23 \\mid ≡1 \\cdot 15 + 8\\begin{vmatrix}(mod15) \\\\ ≡1 \\cdot 0 + 8\\end{vmatrix}(mod15); ≡8 \\mid (mod15)$\n\nAlso, note that $-8 + 8 \\equiv 0$, so $x\\equiv -8\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)$. To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\nx \\mid ≡ - 8\\begin{vmatrix}(mod15) \\\\ ≡0 - 8\\end{vmatrix}(mod15); ≡15 - 8\\begin{vmatrix}(mod15) \\\\ ≡7\\end{vmatrix}(mod15)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $23$ modulo $15$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}7}$.\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n$23 + 7≡30≡2 \\cdot 15≡0✓(mod15)$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n$, i.e. $-a \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: n$." 13194139534236 17592186045417 false] [17592186151867 1074 "The additive inverse of $23$ modulo $15$ is the integer $0 \\leq x \\lt 15$ such that\n\n$$\nx + 23 \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:15).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $23\\equiv 8\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}23 & ≡1\\cdot 15+8 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 0+8 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\  & ≡8 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-8 + 8 \\equiv 0,$ so $x\\equiv -8\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-8 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\  & ≡0-8 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\  & ≡15-8 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\  & ≡7 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $23$ modulo $15$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}7}.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}23+7≡30≡2\\cdot 15≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: n.$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186151868 1071 "Find the additive inverse of $-43$ modulo $15$." 13194139534236 17592186045417 false] [17592186151868 1071 "Find the additive inverse of $-43$ modulo $15.$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186151868 1074 "The additive inverse of $-43$ modulo $15$ is the integer\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\lt 15\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\nx + (-43) \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:15)\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $-43\\equiv 2\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15){:}$\n$-43 \\mid ≡(-3) \\cdot 15 + 2\\begin{vmatrix}(mod15) \\\\ ≡(-3) \\cdot 0 + 2\\end{vmatrix}(mod15); ≡2 \\mid (mod15)$\n\nAlso, note that $-2 + 2 \\equiv 0$, so $x\\equiv -2\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)$. To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\nx \\mid ≡ - 2\\begin{vmatrix}(mod15) \\\\ ≡0 - 2\\end{vmatrix}(mod15); ≡15 - 2\\begin{vmatrix}(mod15) \\\\ ≡13\\end{vmatrix}(mod15)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $-43$ modulo $15$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}13}$.\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n$-43 + 13≡ - 30≡(-2) \\cdot 15≡0✓(mod15)$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n$, i.e. $-a \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: n$." 13194139534236 17592186045417 false] [17592186151868 1074 "The additive inverse of $-43$ modulo $15$ is the integer $0 \\leq x \\lt 15$ such that\n\n$$\nx + (-43) \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:15).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $-43\\equiv 2\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-43 & ≡(-3)\\cdot 15+2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\  & ≡(-3)\\cdot 0+2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\  & ≡2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-2 + 2 \\equiv 0,$ so $x\\equiv -2\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\  & ≡0-2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\  & ≡15-2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\  & ≡13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $-43$ modulo $15$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}13}.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-43+13≡-30≡(-2)\\cdot 15≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: n.$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152062 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415388 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152062 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415391 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152062 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415392 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152062 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415395 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152063 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415386 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152063 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415393 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152063 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415394 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152063 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415400 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152063 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415402 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152064 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415387 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152064 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415397 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152064 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415398 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152064 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415399 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152065 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415389 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152065 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415390 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152065 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415396 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152065 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415401 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152355 1050 420.0 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149518 13194139534236 17592186045417 false] [17592186152491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415385 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152491 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152491 1071 "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $16$, the additive inverse of $45$ modulo $16$ is {{field-1}}." 13194139534236 17592186045417 false] [17592186152491 1071 "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $16,$ the additive inverse of $45$ modulo $16$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152491 1074 "The additive inverse of $45$ modulo $16$ is the integer $0\\le x<16$ such that\n\n$$\nx+45≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $45≡13\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}45 & ≡2\\cdot 16+13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-13+13≡0,$ so $x≡-13\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡0-13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡16-13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡3 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $45$ modulo $16$ is $3.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}45+3≡48≡3\\cdot 16≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152492 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149519 13194139534236 17592186045417 false] [17592186152492 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415363 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152492 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152492 1071 "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $13$, the additive inverse of $-50$ modulo $13$ is {{field-1}}." 13194139534236 17592186045417 false] [17592186152492 1071 "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $13,$ the additive inverse of $-50$ modulo $13$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152492 1074 "The additive inverse of $-50$ modulo $13$ is the integer $0\\le x<13$ such that\n\n$$\nx+(-50)≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $-50≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-50 & ≡(-4)\\cdot 13+2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\  & ≡(-4)\\cdot 0+2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\  & ≡2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-2+2≡0,$ so $x≡-2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\  & ≡0-2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\  & ≡13-2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\  & ≡11 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $-50$ modulo $13$ is $11.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-50+11≡-39≡(-3)\\cdot 13≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152498 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149547 13194139534236 17592186045417 false] [17592186152498 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149550 13194139534236 17592186045417 false] [17592186152498 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415376 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152498 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415383 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152498 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152498 1071 "Select the correct options in the reasoning below." 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186152498 1071 "Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe integer $17$\n{{field-1}}\nthe additive inverse of $12$ modulo $29$ since $17 + 12$ {{field-2}} $(mod29)$." 13194139534236 17592186045417 false] [17592186152498 1074 "The additive inverse of $12$ modulo $29$ is the integer $0\\le x<29$ such that\n\n$$\nx+12≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,29).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that when we add our two numbers, we get a number that is congruent to zero modulo $29:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17+12 & ≡29 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,29) \\\\  & ≡0 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,29)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude the following:\n\nThe integer $17$ $\\text{is}$ the additive inverse of $12$ modulo $29$ since $17+12≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,29).$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186153470 1064 "The **additive inverse** of $a$ modulo $n$ is the integer\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\lt n\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\nx + a \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod} \\: n)\n$$\n\nFor example, the integer $7$ is the additive inverse of $5$ modulo $12$ since\n$7 + 5≡12≡0(mod12)$.\n\nWe can visualize this using a clock diagram.\n\n![](images/5f/5f0c578480e9f9f80820108a0af79ae8eecc826127c6e4d44aa5f3b50a6f086a.png)\n\nOn the other hand, the integer $6$ is *not* the additive inverse of $5$ modulo $12$ since $6 + 5 \\equiv 11\\not\\equiv 0 \\, (\\textrm{mod} \\: 12)$.\n\nIn general, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *For every integer, there exists a unique additive inverse for a given modulus $n$.*\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The word \"unique\" in the statement above means with respect to the inequality $0 \\leq x \\lt n$.\n- It's important to know that any integer summed with an integer congruent to its inverse will also give zero. For example, $x=7$ is the additive inverse of $5$ modulo $12$. But since $19\\equiv 7\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\: 12)$, we have that the sum of $5$ and $19$ is also congruent to $0{:}$\n$19 + 5≡24≡2 \\cdot 12≡0(mod12)$\n\nLet's see some more examples." 13194139534236 17592186045417 false] [17592186153470 1064 "The **additive inverse** of $a$ modulo $n$ is the integer $0 \\leq x \\lt n$ such that\n\n$$\nx + a \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod} \\: n).\n$$\n\nFor example, the integer $7$ is the additive inverse of $5$ modulo $12$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7+5≡12≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can visualize this using a clock diagram.\n\n![](images/5f/5f0c578480e9f9f80820108a0af79ae8eecc826127c6e4d44aa5f3b50a6f086a.png)\n\nOn the other hand, the integer $6$ is *not* the additive inverse of $5$ modulo $12$ since $6 + 5 \\equiv 11\\not\\equiv 0 \\, (\\textrm{mod} \\: 12).$ \n\nIn general, we have the following theorem:\n\n*For every integer, there exists a unique additive inverse for a given modulus $n.$* \n\nNote the following: \n\n- The word \"unique\" in the statement above means with respect to the inequality $0 \\leq x \\lt n.$\n\n- It's important to know that any integer summed with an integer congruent to its inverse will also give zero. \n\nFor example, $x=7$ is the additive inverse of $5$ modulo $12.$ But since $19\\equiv 7\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\: 12),$ we have that the sum of $5$ and $19$ is also congruent to $0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19+5≡24≡2\\cdot 12≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples." 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415316 1080 "≢0" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415316 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415316 1083 #uuid "02b7e8be-bc8e-5072-b1ee-98569da2d2f6" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415317 1080 "≡0" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415317 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415317 1083 #uuid "06ae9272-d8f2-5789-9c55-fc770364606e" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415318 1080 "≢0" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415318 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415318 1083 #uuid "07c38606-0ce2-548e-b795-0e49ddaaefe7" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415319 1080 "21" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415319 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415319 1083 #uuid "0b1b9b33-ec22-5e04-89e8-0714d1ed63d8" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415320 1080 "is" 13194