34236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415386 1069 "q-213385" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415386 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415386 1071 "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $35,$ the additive inverse of $23$ modulo $35$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415386 1072 #uuid "0244a7b0-af5a-5462-8463-f5b7c25a67e5" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415386 1074 "The additive inverse of $23$ modulo $35$ is the integer $0\\le x<35$ such that\n\n$$\nx+23≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,35).\n$$\n\nNotice that $-23+23≡0,$ so $x≡-23\\,(\\text{mod}\\,35).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-23 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,35) \\\\  & ≡0-23 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,35) \\\\  & ≡35-23 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,35) \\\\  & ≡12 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $23$ modulo $35$ is $12.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n12+23≡35≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,35)\n$$\n\n**Note:** The additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415374 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415387 1069 "q-213345" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415387 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415387 1071 "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $8,$ the additive inverse of $19$ modulo $8$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415387 1072 #uuid "03d7746d-d7c7-51a7-a5bf-9cade8e1e535" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415387 1074 "The additive inverse of $19$ modulo $8$ is the integer $0\\le x<8$ such that\n\n$$\nx+19≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $19≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19 & ≡2\\cdot 8+3 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+3 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡3 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-3+3≡0,$ so $x≡-3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-3 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡0-3 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡8-3 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡5 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $19$ modulo $8$ is $5.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19+5≡24≡3\\cdot 8≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415377 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415380 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415388 1069 "q-213365" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415388 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415388 1071 "Select the correct options in the reasoning below." 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415388 1072 #uuid "06e3421b-201f-5b72-a27d-eae8c015169e" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415388 1074 "The additive inverse of $18$ modulo $48$ is the integer $0\\le x<48$ such that\n\n$$\nx+18≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,48).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that when we add our two numbers, we get a number that is *not* congruent to zero modulo $48:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}34+18 & ≡52 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,48) \\\\  & ≡48+4 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,48) \\\\  & ≡4 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,48) \\\\  & ≢0 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,48)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude the following:\n\nThe integer $34$ $\\text{is not}$ the additive inverse of $18$ modulo $48$ since $34+18≢0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,48).$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415389 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415381 13194139534236