ce $38+26≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,64).$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415393 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415378 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415393 1069 "q-213391" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415393 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415393 1071 "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $42,$ the additive inverse of $29$ modulo $42$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415393 1072 #uuid "3323609a-bf83-5586-b0db-68ed80775b56" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415393 1074 "The additive inverse of $29$ modulo $42$ is the integer $0\\le x<42$ such that\n\n$$\nx+29≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,42).\n$$\n\nNotice that $-29+29≡0,$ so $x≡-29\\,(\\text{mod}\\,32).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-29 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,42) \\\\  & ≡0-29 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,42) \\\\  & ≡42-29 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,42) \\\\  & ≡13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,42)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $19$ modulo $42$ is $13.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n13+29≡42≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,42)\n$$\n\n**Note:** The additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415369 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415394 1069 "q-213382" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415394 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415394 1071 "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $22,$ the additive inverse of $14$ modulo $22$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415394 1072 #uuid "57513638-55f0-5e26-b8a8-dbc4daee54c1" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415394 1074 "The additive inverse of $14$ modulo $22$ is the integer $0\\le x<22$ such that\n\n$$\nx+14≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,22).\n$$\n\nNotice that $-14+14≡0,$ so $x≡-14\\,(\\text{mod}\\,22).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-14 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,22) \\\\  & ≡0-14 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,22) \\\\  & ≡22-14 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,22) \\\\  & ≡8 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,22)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $14$ modulo $22$ is $8.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n8+14≡22≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,22)\n$$\n\n**Note:** The additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415368 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415382 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415395 1069 "q-213368" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415395 1071 "Select the correct options in the reasoning below." 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415395 1072 #uuid "60b1fda4-60e8-5732-9f6d-dacad47edd54" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415395 1074 "The additive inverse of $13$ modulo $37$ is the integer $0\\le x<37$ such that\n\n$$\nx+13≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,37).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that when we add our two numbers, we get a number that is *not* congruent to zero modulo $37:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14+13 & ≡27 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,37) \\\\  & ≢0 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,37)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude the following:\n\nThe integer $14$ $\\text{is not}$ the additive inverse of $13$ modulo $27$ since $14+13≢0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,37).$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415379 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415396 1069 "q-213355" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415396 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415396 1071 "Exp