$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & ≡1\\cdot 9+6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 0+6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-6+6≡0,$ so $x≡-6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡0-6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡9-6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡3 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $15$ modulo $9$ is $3.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15+3≡18≡2\\cdot 9≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415366 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415398 1069 "q-213340" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415398 1071 "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $16,$ the additive inverse of $28$ modulo $16$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415398 1072 #uuid "71d1db00-def0-5962-9ebf-e72a766990a9" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415398 1074 "The additive inverse of $28$ modulo $16$ is the integer $0\\le x<16$ such that\n\n$$\nx+28≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $28≡12\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & ≡1\\cdot 16+12 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 0+12 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡12 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-12+12≡0,$ so $x≡-12\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-12 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡0-12 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡16-12 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  