7592186045417 true] [17592186415400 1074 "The additive inverse of $7$ modulo $28$ is the integer $0\\le x<28$ such that\n\n$$\nx+7≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,28).\n$$\n\nNotice that $-7+7≡0,$ so $x≡-7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,28).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-7 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,28) \\\\  & ≡0-7 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,28) \\\\  & ≡28-7 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,28) \\\\  & ≡21 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,28)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $7$ modulo $28$ is $21.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n7+21≡28≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,28)\n$$\n\n**Note:** The additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415362 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415401 1069 "q-213354" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415401 1071 "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $23,$ the additive inverse of $-49$ modulo $23$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415401 1072 #uuid "c9d728b0-5a87-57b1-8b30-46d4db7caf62" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415401 1074 "The additive inverse of $-49$ modulo $23$ is the integer $0\\le x<23$ such that\n\n$$\nx+(-49)≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,23).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $-49≡20\\,(\\text{mod}\\,23):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-49 & ≡(-3)\\cdot 23+20 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡(-3)\\cdot 0+20 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡20 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,23)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-20+20≡0,$ so $x≡-20\\,(\\text{mod}\\,23).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-20 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡0-20 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡23-20 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡3 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,23)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $-49$ modulo $23$ is $3.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-49+3≡-46≡(-2)\\cdot 23≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,23)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415402 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415373 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415402 1069 "q-213387" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415402 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415402 1071 "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $38,$ the additive inverse of $16$ modulo $38$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415402 1072 #uuid "f130e5d0-ce45-5587-8424-e7b9465649e8" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true] [17592186415402 1074 "The additive inverse of $16$ modulo $38$ is the integer $0\\le x<38$ such that\n\n$$\nx+16≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,38).\n$$\n\nNotice that $-16+16≡0,$ so $x≡-16\\,(\\text{mod}\\,38).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-16 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,38) \\\\  & ≡0-16 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,38) \\\\  & ≡38-16 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,38) \\\\  & ≡22 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,38)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $16$ modulo $38$ is $22.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n22+16≡38≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,38)\n$$\n\n**Note:** The additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$" 13194139534236 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-02b7e8be-bc8e-5072-b1ee-98569da2d2f6" 17592186415316 "answer-06ae9272-d8f2-5789-9c55-fc770364606e" 17592186415317 "answer-07c38606-0ce2-548e-b795-0e49ddaaefe7" 17592186415318 "answer-0b1b9b33-ec22-5e04-89e8-0714d1ed63d8" 17592186415319 "answer-0d581af0-d00d-53b6-a75d-880354061dae